# Your TypeScript harness agent

This starter was bundled with @temporalio/agent-harness __HARNESS_VERSION__. It needs Node 22.18+,
npm, uv, the Temporal CLI, and an OpenAI API key. Its weather tool returns sample data.

```bash
npm install
cp env.example .env.local
# Set OPENAI_API_KEY in .env.local; never commit the key.
npm run schema
npm run skill
```

Run these from this directory in separate terminals:

```bash
temporal server start-dev
uvx --from 'temporal-agent-harness[ui]==0.8.0' temporal-agent-harness session-manager
uvx --from 'temporal-agent-harness[ui]==0.8.0' temporal-agent-harness serve agents.toml
npm start
```

Skip the first command when your configured Temporal service already exists. The default is
localhost:7233, namespace default. Export identical TEMPORAL_* settings in every service terminal;
`.env.local` loads only into the Node worker. Leave TASK_QUEUE_PREFIX unset for this starter.
The API, console and session-manager worker are Python; your agent worker runs in Node.

Open http://localhost:8000, start OpenAI Hello (TypeScript), and send `ask` a weather question.
Expand the tool trace to see get_weather and its sample result. `ping` echoes a typed text message
without calling a model. Reload the page to reconnect. Temporal's own UI is at localhost:8233.

Run `npm run doctor` for read-only diagnostics. After changing the agent, run `npm run schema`
and restart `npm start`; use a new session for changed workflow behavior. Commit the schema and
package-lock.json. For application clients, consult the installed skill's references/api.md.
